    "created_at": "2020-07-08T18:27:30Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-07-08T18:27:58Z",
    "body": "> As a compromise you could use ghc -- --numeric-version to get the global ghc version.\r\n\r\nI played around with this on my MacBook a few weeks back and took a look again just now--`ghc -- --numeric-version` takes almost 150ms when run cold, and speeds up to about 110ms when run repeatedly in a short time window (I assume some sort of MacOS caching artifact). These are show-stopping times for a language module.\r\n\r\n> it could look for the presence of a stack.yaml | package.yaml\r\n\r\nSo about that...I looked into the `stack` source code, and while I haven't read or written Haskell in quite a while, I thought it looked like Stack was reading in some information from a config file, then attempting to get information via an SQL connector. Did I understand that correctly, or was there some `Alternative` stuff that I missed? If so, why does it need to do that? On my MacBook (an older model, but by no means a rare one), this seems to shoot the runtimes sky-high, from 100-150ms for running ghc directly to almost 400ms to get the haskell version from stack (via a `stack run` or `stack ghc` command).",
